\begin{Verbatim}[commandchars=\\\{\}]
\PYG{k+kd}{const}\PYG{+w}{ }\PYG{n+nx}{CC\PYGZus{}LIMIT\PYGZus{}TEXT}\PYG{o}{=}\PYG{p}{\PYGZob{}}
\PYG{+w}{ }\PYG{n+nx}{heat\PYGZus{}rate}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{p}{\PYGZob{}}\PYG{n+nx}{lo}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{l+s+s2}{\PYGZdq{}Heating is slower than usual. A single low run: plate not seated, wrong plate type, blocked air vents or a warm room that day. A slow fall over many runs: Peltier elements or thermal paste ageing — plan a thermal service before the specification (4.3 °C/s on the LightCycler) is reached.\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{,}\PYG{n+nx}{hi}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{l+s+s2}{\PYGZdq{}Heating is faster than usual. Usually harmless: after a service, a new block or a firmware change. If isolated, check the run used the same protocol and plate; very fast ramps with a larger overshoot can stress samples.\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{\PYGZcb{},}
\PYG{+w}{ }\PYG{n+nx}{cool\PYGZus{}rate}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{p}{\PYGZob{}}\PYG{n+nx}{lo}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{l+s+s2}{\PYGZdq{}Cooling is slower than usual: the heat sink is not getting rid of heat. First clean the fan filters and check that all fans run and the vents are free; then check room temperature. If cooling keeps falling while heating stays normal after cleaning, the Peltier/heat\PYGZhy{}sink contact needs service.\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{,}\PYG{n+nx}{hi}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{l+s+s2}{\PYGZdq{}Cooling is faster than usual. Typical after cleaning the filters, a fan replacement or a service, or in a cooler room. Note the event and, if it was a service, start a new baseline on that date.\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{\PYGZcb{},}
\PYG{+w}{ }\PYG{n+nx}{overshoot}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{p}{\PYGZob{}}\PYG{n+nx}{lo}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{l+s+s2}{\PYGZdq{}The block overshoots less than it used to (CUSUM drifting down). The temperature controller or the sensor calibration has changed — typical after a service or recalibration. Check that the reading temperature (B\PYGZhy{}H4) did not shift at the same time.\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{,}\PYG{n+nx}{hi}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{l+s+s2}{\PYGZdq{}The block overshoots more than it used to (CUSUM drifting up). The controller is compensating for a slower block, or the sensor is ageing. Samples briefly see higher temperatures; check heating rate (B\PYGZhy{}H1) and plan a temperature verification.\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{\PYGZcb{},}
\PYG{+w}{ }\PYG{n+nx}{settling}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{p}{\PYGZob{}}\PYG{n+nx}{lo}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{l+s+s2}{\PYGZdq{}The block reaches a stable temperature faster than usual. Harmless in itself; if it coincides with a step in overshoot and offset, the controller was re\PYGZhy{}tuned or recalibrated (start a new baseline).\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{,}\PYG{n+nx}{hi}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{l+s+s2}{\PYGZdq{}The block needs longer to settle. With normal ramp rates: control tuning or sensor lag. With slower ramps as well: thermal ageing. Long settling shortens the real hold time at the setpoint and can lower efficiency.\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{\PYGZcb{},}
\PYG{+w}{ }\PYG{n+nx}{transition}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{p}{\PYGZob{}}\PYG{n+nx}{lo}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{l+s+s2}{\PYGZdq{}The block moves between these two temperatures faster than usual: after a service, a cleaned fan filter or a cooler room. Harmless; if it coincides with a step in overshoot or offset, start a new baseline on the service date.\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{,}\PYG{n+nx}{hi}\PYG{o}{:}\PYG{l+s+s2}{\PYGZdq{}The block needs longer to move between these two temperatures, so every cycle spends less time at the programmed hold. A cooling transition (e.g. 95 → 60 °C) getting longer = heat removal (clean fan filters, check fans, room temperature); heating transitions getting longer = Peltier ageing. Samples spend more time at intermediate temperatures, which can favour non\PYGZhy{}specific annealing.\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{\PYGZcb{},}
\end{Verbatim}
